# Per-machine session customisation. Replaces ~/.xsessionrc from the X11 setup.
#
# Sourced at the start of every graphical session, after your home directory
# has been restored and before the ThinLinc client starts. Because this file
# lives in your home directory it is saved by /usr/bin/dbrrg-save-home and
# restored on the next boot, so changes persist without rebuilding the image.
#
# After editing, quit the ThinLinc client once to save your home - otherwise
# the change is lost at the next reboot.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DISPLAYS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# wlr-randr replaces xrandr on this system. List what is connected with:
#
#   wlr-randr
#
# Rotate the second screen 90 degrees and place it to the right of a 1920-wide
# primary screen (use the output names wlr-randr printed):
#
#   wlr-randr --output DP-1 --transform 90 --pos 1920,0
#
# Transforms are: normal, 90, 180, 270, flipped, flipped-90, flipped-180,
# flipped-270.
#
# Position matters: the ThinLinc client sizes itself to the union of all
# outputs, so where you place a rotated screen determines the shape of the
# remote desktop. --pos is x,y of the output's top-left corner.
#
# Set a mode explicitly if the default is wrong:
#
#   wlr-randr --output HDMI-A-1 --mode 1920x1080@60
#
# If a monitor is switched on after boot, or wakes from standby, a one-shot
# wlr-randr will not be reapplied. For that, use kanshi instead - it watches
# for hotplug and reasserts the layout. Write a profile to
# ~/.config/kanshi/config and start it here:
#
#   kanshi -c "$HOME/.config/kanshi/config" &
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KEYBOARD - set it in ~/.dbrrg-environment, not here
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Setting XKB_DEFAULT_LAYOUT here has NO EFFECT: the compositor reads those
# variables when it starts, and this file is sourced from inside the
# already-running session.
#
# Use ~/.dbrrg-environment instead. That file is sourced before the compositor
# launches and is saved and restored with your home in exactly the same way.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NETWORK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WiFi, as in the old setup - edit ~/wifi.yaml first, then:
#
#   sudo cp wifi.yaml /etc/netplan/
#   sudo chown root /etc/netplan/*
#   sudo chmod 400 /etc/netplan/*
#   sudo netplan apply
#
